# Every tracked file's line endings are governed here, so a clean checkout never reports
# modifications and no file's stored bytes depend on the committing developer's core.autocrlf.
# Text files are auto-detected, stored as LF, and checked out as LF on every platform; eol
# overrides core.autocrlf, so a Windows checkout gets LF too. A working tree created before this
# line needs a one-time refresh: re-clone, or `git rm --cached -r . && git reset --hard` on a
# clean tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries, marked explicitly rather than left to auto-detection.
*.png -text
*.jpg -text
*.snk -text

# Verify snapshots are byte-exact records of program output; UTF-8 in the working tree keeps them
# comparable across platforms.
*.verified.txt text eol=lf working-tree-encoding=UTF-8
*.verified.xml text eol=lf working-tree-encoding=UTF-8
*.verified.json text eol=lf working-tree-encoding=UTF-8

# Generated data, written only by scripts/pr-review-costs.sh: GitHub collapses it in pull request
# diffs and leaves it out of language statistics, so a refresh does not bury the change it rides with.
docs/metrics/pr-review-costs.md linguist-generated=true

# Forced text (not merely auto-detected): a shell script or a source file with LF endings is a
# correctness requirement, not a preference.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.cs text eol=lf
